# A loop that walks a list from its first element starts its counter at one
# and only ever adds to it, and every call to it is in sight. The counter is
# then at least one at every read, so the guard on the far end is the whole
# proof, and `xs[at]` hands `double` an element, never a none.
fn double n
  n * 2

fn total xs at acc
  return acc if length xs < at
  total xs (at + 1) (acc + double xs[at])

pub play = print "{total [1 2 3] 1 0}"
